WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.400
In 2013, a laboratory in Vienna grew folded tissue in a dish.

2
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:15.740
It came from the skin cells of a person with severe microcephaly, a brain far

3
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:16.820
smaller than normal.

4
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.640
The zones of dividing cortical progenitors in that tissue were smaller than in

5
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.020
tissue from control cells.

6
00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.840
Could skin cells become a model of early cortex?

7
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.540
And how far can that model be trusted?

8
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:35.713
First, the skin cell has to return to an embryonic state.

9
00:00:36.753 --> 00:00:38.733
Four transcription factors can do that.

10
00:00:39.653 --> 00:00:44.793
Transcription factors are proteins that bind DNA and turn genes on or off.

11
00:00:45.673 --> 00:00:50.593
In human skin cells, the four factors made induced pluripotent stem cells.

12
00:00:51.713 --> 00:00:55.593
Pluripotent means able to give rise to every cell type of the body.

13
00:00:55.593 --> 00:01:01.413
A line made from a patient carries the patient's whole genome, mutation included.

14
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:07.693
Next, the cells are gathered into a ball and steered toward neural tissue.

15
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:10.893
The ball is embedded in a droplet of gel.

16
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:16.953
Then it goes into a spinning flask, where moving medium improves the supply of

17
00:01:16.953 --> 00:01:18.193
oxygen and nutrients.

18
00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:20.693
The cells arrange themselves.

19
00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:26.193
That is self-organization, and it is the idea behind every organoid.

20
00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:30.807
Inside, regions form that look like early cortex.

21
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:34.507
Radial glia surround a fluid-filled cavity.

22
00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:39.847
Their nuclei move toward the cavity and away again as the cells divide.

23
00:01:41.327 --> 00:01:45.687
Beyond them sit intermediate progenitors, and further out, early neurons.

24
00:01:46.907 --> 00:01:51.567
A comparison with developing human cortex found the same broad classes of cells.

25
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:54.087
The match has limits.

26
00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:59.107
Organoids have no blood vessels, so the center of a large one dies.

27
00:02:00.387 --> 00:02:05.907
Finer cell subtypes blur: organoid cells often resemble several subtypes in

28
00:02:05.907 --> 00:02:07.727
developing cortex about equally.

29
00:02:09.287 --> 00:02:14.187
Stress genes run higher in organoid cells, and they fell after transplant into mouse

30
00:02:14.187 --> 00:02:17.507
cortex, which suggests the culture environment contributes.

31
00:02:17.507 --> 00:02:23.087
And organoids model early and mid-fetal stages, not a mature brain.

32
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:29.540
One patient's organoids could be smaller because of the gene, the rest of that

33
00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:33.780
person's genes, a quirk of one cell line, or a bad batch.

34
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:39.060
So use several clones from the patient and several controls from different people,

35
00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.740
grown side by side, more than once.

36
00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.760
Then put the gene back into patient tissue, or remove it from a control, and see

37
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:48.960
whether the result follows.

38
00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:55.040
Resemblance has to be measured, and a mutation is blamed only after the controls.
